      "content": "that makes sense. i've already started setting up the first few nodes with some high-sensitivity induction coils. one thing i'm running into, though, is the signal-to-noise ratio. whenever i get close to a transformer or a major distribution point, the standard 60hz harmonic content is so massive it's completely drowning out the sub-harmonic modulation i'm looking for. \n\nis there a way to use a notch filter or some kind of phase-locked loop (PLL) configuration to \"null out\" the primary 60hz carrier in real-time? i need to isolate that specific sub-harmonic signature so i don't get false positives from the standard grid noise. if i can pull the carrier out of the way, i should be able to see the underlying modulation pattern much more clearly, right?",
